/* Pharmacology 6: CNS */
Z("pharm6",0,"ph",["Sedative-hypnotics calm anxiety (sedation) and induce sleep (hypnosis); benzodiazepines like diazepam have replaced barbiturates because they are much safer in overdose.","Benzodiazepines turn up the volume of the brain's natural 'calm' signal (GABA) only when GABA is present; barbiturates can open the calm gate on their own — hence far more dangerous.",["Classification: barbiturates (phenobarbitone, thiopentone), benzodiazepines (diazepam, lorazepam, alprazolam, midazolam, clonazepam, nitrazepam), Z-drugs (zolpidem, zaleplon, eszopiclone), others (melatonin agonists — ramelteon; orexin antagonists — suvorexant).","Diazepam MOA: binds BZD site on GABA-A receptor → ↑ frequency of Cl⁻ channel opening (barbiturates ↑ duration; high-dose direct opening).","Diazepam uses: anxiety, insomnia (short), status epilepticus and febrile convulsions, alcohol withdrawal, muscle spasm/tetanus, preanaesthetic/sedation, ECT/endoscopy, eclampsia (alternative).","Diazepam PK: well absorbed, highly lipophilic, active metabolites (desmethyldiazepam — long t½); ADR — drowsiness, ataxia, anterograde amnesia, dependence, withdrawal; overdose — antidote flumazenil."],"BZD ↑ Frequency; Barbiturate ↑ Duration ('BBF: Barbs Duration, Benzos Frequency')."],
"Sedatives reduce excitement and anxiety and calm the patient; hypnotics induce and maintain sleep resembling natural sleep. Classification: (1) barbiturates — long-acting: phenobarbitone, mephobarbitone; short-acting: butobarbitone, pentobarbitone; ultra-short-acting: thiopentone, methohexitone; (2) benzodiazepines — hypnotic: diazepam, flurazepam, nitrazepam, alprazolam, temazepam, triazolam, flunitrazepam; antianxiety: diazepam, chlordiazepoxide, oxazepam, lorazepam, alprazolam, clobazam; anticonvulsant: diazepam, clonazepam, lorazepam, clobazam, midazolam; preanaesthetic/IV anaesthesia: midazolam, diazepam, lorazepam; (3) non-benzodiazepine hypnotics (Z-drugs) — zolpidem, zaleplon, zopiclone, eszopiclone (act at BZ1/α1 subtype); (4) melatonin receptor agonists — ramelteon, tasimelteon, melatonin; (5) orexin receptor antagonists — suvorexant, lemborexant; (6) others — chloral hydrate, triclofos, sedating antihistamines, antidepressants (trazodone, mirtazapine), dexmedetomidine. Mechanism: benzodiazepines bind to a specific allosteric site at the α/γ subunit interface of the GABA-A receptor–chloride channel complex, increasing the affinity of GABA and the frequency of channel opening, causing hyperpolarisation; they do not act without GABA (ceiling effect, safer). Barbiturates bind a different site, increase the duration of channel opening, and at high doses directly open the channel and block AMPA/kainate glutamate receptors — no ceiling, hence severe respiratory depression. Diazepam: actions — anxiolysis, sedation, hypnosis (reduces sleep latency, increases total sleep, reduces REM and stage 3–4 sleep), anterograde amnesia, anticonvulsant, centrally mediated muscle relaxation (spinal polysynaptic reflexes), minimal respiratory and cardiovascular depression at therapeutic doses (except IV or with other depressants); pharmacokinetics — rapidly and completely absorbed orally (rapid onset), erratic IM absorption, highly lipophilic (rapid CNS entry and redistribution), 98% protein bound, metabolised by CYP2C19/3A4 to active desmethyldiazepam (t½ 50–100 h), oxazepam and temazepam; diazepam t½ 20–80 h (prolonged in elderly and liver disease); available orally, IV, rectally (tube or gel — useful for febrile seizures in children); uses — anxiety disorders (short term), insomnia (short term), status epilepticus (IV 10 mg; lorazepam preferred), febrile convulsions (rectal), tetanus and strychnine poisoning, local anaesthetic toxicity and drug-induced seizures, alcohol withdrawal syndrome (prevent DTs), muscle spasm and spasticity, preanaesthetic medication and conscious sedation for endoscopy/cardioversion (midazolam preferred), panic disorder (alprazolam, clonazepam), eclampsia (alternative to magnesium), vertigo; adverse effects — drowsiness, impaired psychomotor performance (driving), ataxia, confusion and falls in elderly, anterograde amnesia, paradoxical excitement, tolerance and dependence with withdrawal (anxiety, insomnia, tremor, seizures), respiratory depression with IV use or with alcohol/opioids, thrombophlebitis (IV), teratogenic concerns (floppy infant syndrome when given near term). Overdose of benzodiazepines alone is rarely fatal; treated supportively and with flumazenil (competitive BZD antagonist; short t½; may precipitate seizures in mixed overdoses or dependent patients).",
[["Benzodiazepines vs barbiturates",[["Feature","Benzodiazepines","Barbiturates"],["Mechanism","↑ Frequency of GABA-A Cl⁻ channel opening","↑ Duration; direct opening at high doses"],["Ceiling effect","Yes","No"],["Respiratory depression","Minimal (therapeutic)","Marked, dose-dependent"],["Therapeutic index","High","Low"],["Enzyme induction","No","Yes (interactions)"],["REM suppression","Less","More, marked rebound"],["Dependence and abuse","Lower","Higher"],["Withdrawal","Milder","Severe (seizures, delirium)"],["Antidote","Flumazenil","None"],["Porphyria","Safe","Contraindicated"]]],
["Diazepam uses",["Anxiety disorders (short-term)","Insomnia","Status epilepticus, febrile convulsions","Alcohol withdrawal","Tetanus, muscle spasm","Preanaesthetic, sedation for procedures","Eclampsia (alternative)"]],
["Adverse effects",["Drowsiness, ataxia, impaired driving","Anterograde amnesia","Falls and confusion in elderly","Tolerance, dependence, withdrawal","Respiratory depression with alcohol/opioids or IV","Floppy infant syndrome"]]],
{i:"Benzodiazepines are the most widely used sedative-hypnotics; their safety advantage over barbiturates stems from GABA-dependent action.",f:"GABA → GABA-A receptor (Cl⁻ channel)\n BZD (α/γ site) → ↑ frequency of opening\n Barbiturate (β site) → ↑ duration, direct opening (high dose)\n → Cl⁻ influx → hyperpolarisation → sedation, hypnosis, anticonvulsant, muscle relaxation\n Flumazenil reverses BZD only",c:"Benzodiazepines such as diazepam are safer than barbiturates because their effect depends on GABA; they should be used short-term to avoid tolerance and dependence."});

Z("pharm6",1,"ph",["Pralidoxime (2-PAM) is a cholinesterase reactivator used in organophosphate poisoning; it pulls the organophosphate off the enzyme so acetylcholinesterase can work again, if given before the enzyme 'ages'.","OP sticks to the enzyme like glue; pralidoxime is a solvent that dissolves the glue — but only while it is still wet (before ageing).",["Purpose: reactivate phosphorylated AChE by nucleophilic attack on phosphorus (oxime group).","Most effective at nicotinic sites (NM junction) — reverses muscle weakness; little effect on muscarinic and CNS symptoms (atropine needed).","Give early (within 24–48 h, before ageing); dose 30 mg/kg IV bolus then 8 mg/kg/h infusion (WHO).","Not useful in carbamate poisoning (spontaneous reactivation; may worsen carbaryl); ADR — dizziness, blurred vision, tachycardia, NM block at high doses. Others: obidoxime, diacetylmonoxime (crosses BBB)."],"PAM = 'Pulls OP off AChE — Pronto (before ageing)'."],
"Pralidoxime (2-PAM, pyridine-2-aldoxime methiodide/chloride) is a cholinesterase reactivator used as an antidote, along with atropine, in organophosphate (OP) poisoning. Purpose and mechanism: organophosphates phosphorylate the serine hydroxyl group at the esteratic site of acetylcholinesterase, inactivating it; the oxime group of pralidoxime has high affinity for the phosphorus atom and, attaching to the anionic site, makes a nucleophilic attack, removing the phosphoryl group as an oxime–phosphonate complex, thereby regenerating free active enzyme. Pralidoxime also directly inactivates (detoxifies) unbound OP in plasma. Limitations: the phosphorylated enzyme undergoes ageing (dealkylation) within minutes to hours depending on the compound (soman in 2 minutes, others 24–48 h), after which the enzyme cannot be reactivated — hence oximes must be given early; pralidoxime is a quaternary compound that does not cross the BBB, so it acts mainly at peripheral sites, especially nicotinic neuromuscular junctions (reverses muscle weakness and paralysis), with limited effect on muscarinic and central symptoms, which require atropine; ineffective against carbamate anticholinesterases (carbamylated enzyme reactivates spontaneously; oximes may worsen carbaryl poisoning) and not useful for OP-induced delayed neuropathy. Dose (WHO): 30 mg/kg IV over 20–30 minutes, then infusion of 8 mg/kg/h (or 1–2 g every 4–6 h) until atropine is no longer needed and patient improves. Evidence of clinical benefit in human OP poisoning is mixed but it is recommended especially for agents with slow ageing. Adverse effects: dizziness, blurred vision, diplopia, headache, nausea, tachycardia, hypertension, muscle weakness and neuromuscular block at high doses, laryngospasm with rapid IV injection. Other oximes: obidoxime (more potent), diacetylmonoxime (DAM — tertiary, crosses BBB), HI-6 (nerve agents).",
[["Key points",["Reactivates phosphorylated AChE (oxime nucleophilic attack)","Give early — before ageing","Acts at nicotinic NM junction; atropine still essential","Not for carbamates","Dose: 30 mg/kg IV then 8 mg/kg/h"]]],
{c:"Pralidoxime complements atropine in organophosphate poisoning by reactivating cholinesterase at the neuromuscular junction, provided it is given before enzyme ageing."});

Z("pharm6",2,"ph",["Methanol poisoning (from spurious liquor, solvents) is dangerous because methanol is converted in the liver to formaldehyde and formic acid, causing severe acidosis and blindness; treatment blocks its metabolism with fomepizole or ethanol and removes it by dialysis.","Methanol itself is not very toxic; the liver turns it into poison (formic acid). Ethanol or fomepizole occupy the converting enzyme so methanol is excreted unchanged.",["Toxic metabolites: methanol → (alcohol dehydrogenase) formaldehyde → (aldehyde dehydrogenase) formic acid → metabolic acidosis with high anion gap, optic nerve damage.","Features after 12–24 h latency: headache, vomiting, abdominal pain, blurred vision, 'snowstorm' vision, blindness, Kussmaul breathing, coma; putaminal necrosis.","Treatment: fomepizole (ADH inhibitor) or ethanol (competitive substrate, maintain 100–150 mg/dL), sodium bicarbonate to correct acidosis, folinic/folic acid (enhances formate breakdown), haemodialysis.","Supportive: gastric lavage only within 1 h, airway, fluids; avoid disulfiram."],"Methanol antidotes: 'Fomepizole/Ethanol + Bicarbonate + Folate + Dialysis'."],
"Methyl alcohol (methanol, wood spirit) poisoning occurs from adulterated illicit liquor (hooch tragedies in India), industrial solvents, antifreeze and paint thinners. Methanol itself has low toxicity; it is slowly oxidised in the liver by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde, and by aldehyde dehydrogenase to formic acid, which is responsible for toxicity — it inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase, causing histotoxic hypoxia, severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis (with lactate), and injury to the optic nerve and retina (retinal oedema, optic disc hyperaemia, optic atrophy) and basal ganglia (putamen necrosis). Formate is slowly converted to CO2 by a folate-dependent pathway (limited in humans). Clinical features: after a latent period of 12–24 hours (longer if ethanol was co-ingested) — mild inebriation, headache, vertigo, nausea, vomiting, severe abdominal pain (pancreatitis), breathlessness (Kussmaul), blurred vision, photophobia, 'snowstorm' vision, dilated unreactive pupils, blindness, restlessness, delirium, convulsions, coma, hypotension, death from respiratory failure; survivors may have permanent blindness and parkinsonism. Investigations: high anion gap metabolic acidosis, raised osmolal gap, serum methanol and formate levels, CT brain. Treatment: (1) supportive — airway, breathing, circulation; gastric lavage within 1 hour (activated charcoal ineffective); (2) correction of acidosis — IV sodium bicarbonate generously (keeps formate ionised, reducing its entry into tissues; maintain pH > 7.3); (3) inhibition of metabolism — fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole; competitive ADH inhibitor; 15 mg/kg IV loading then 10 mg/kg 12-hourly) or ethanol (higher affinity for ADH; 10% IV or 40% oral loading 0.8 g/kg, maintenance to keep blood ethanol 100–150 mg/dL) — continued until methanol < 20 mg/dL; (4) enhancement of formate metabolism — folinic acid (leucovorin) or folic acid 50 mg IV 4–6 hourly; (5) elimination — haemodialysis removes methanol and formate, corrects acidosis; indicated in severe acidosis (pH < 7.25), visual impairment, renal failure, methanol level > 50 mg/dL, deteriorating condition; (6) correction of hypoglycaemia, electrolytes; protect eyes from light. Ethylene glycol poisoning is managed similarly (with thiamine and pyridoxine).",
[["Metabolism",["Methanol → (ADH) → formaldehyde → (ALDH) → formic acid → (folate) → CO2 + H2O","Formic acid: acidosis, retinal/optic nerve and putamen toxicity"]],
["Treatment",[["Measure","Purpose"],["Fomepizole / ethanol","Block ADH, prevent formic acid formation"],["Sodium bicarbonate","Correct acidosis, ↓ formate tissue entry"],["Folinic/folic acid","↑ Formate oxidation"],["Haemodialysis","Remove methanol and formate"],["Supportive care","Airway, fluids, glucose"]]]],
{c:"Methanol poisoning is treated by blocking its conversion to formic acid, correcting acidosis, giving folate and dialysis; early treatment prevents blindness and death."});

Z("pharm6",3,"ph",["Antiepileptic drugs control seizures by reducing excessive neuronal firing — blocking sodium or calcium channels, enhancing GABA inhibition or reducing glutamate excitation; key drugs are phenytoin, carbamazepine and sodium valproate.","They act as brakes on overexcited neurons: some plug the sodium channels (phenytoin, carbamazepine), some boost GABA, valproate does several things at once.",["Classification: hydantoins (phenytoin, fosphenytoin), barbiturates (phenobarbitone), iminostilbenes (carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine), succinimides (ethosuximide), valproate, BZDs (clonazepam, clobazam, diazepam, lorazepam), newer (lamotrigine, levetiracetam, topiramate, gabapentin, pregabalin, lacosamide, vigabatrin, zonisamide).","Phenytoin: prolongs inactivation of Na⁺ channels; zero-order kinetics at therapeutic levels (TDM 10–20 µg/mL), enzyme inducer; ADR — gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, coarse facies, ataxia, nystagmus, megaloblastic anaemia, osteomalacia, fetal hydantoin syndrome, lymphadenopathy, SJS, purple glove; uses — GTCS, focal, status epilepticus.","Carbamazepine: Na⁺ channel block; autoinduction; ADR — diplopia, ataxia, hyponatraemia (SIADH), agranulocytosis/aplastic anaemia, SJS (HLA-B*1502), teratogen; uses — focal seizures (drug of choice), GTCS, trigeminal neuralgia, bipolar.","Valproate: Na⁺ block, ↑ GABA (GABA-T inhibition), T-type Ca²⁺ block; broad spectrum — absence, myoclonic, GTCS, focal, bipolar, migraine; ADR — hepatotoxicity (children < 2), pancreatitis, weight gain, tremor, hair loss, PCOS, thrombocytopenia, NTD (most teratogenic), enzyme inhibitor."],"Valproate = 'broad spectrum' but avoid in pregnancy; Carbamazepine = focal + trigeminal neuralgia."],
"Antiepileptic (antiseizure) drugs suppress seizures by reducing neuronal excitability. Mechanisms: (1) prolongation of inactivated state of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels (phenytoin, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, lamotrigine, valproate, topiramate, lacosamide — slow inactivation); (2) enhancement of GABA-mediated inhibition — GABA-A receptor modulation (benzodiazepines — frequency, barbiturates — duration), inhibition of GABA transaminase (vigabatrin, valproate), GABA reuptake inhibition (tiagabine); (3) blockade of T-type Ca²⁺ channels in thalamus (ethosuximide, valproate, zonisamide) — absence seizures; (4) α2δ subunit of Ca²⁺ channels (gabapentin, pregabalin); (5) reduction of glutamate excitation — AMPA antagonism (perampanel, topiramate), NMDA (felbamate); (6) SV2A synaptic vesicle protein binding (levetiracetam, brivaracetam); (7) carbonic anhydrase inhibition (acetazolamide, topiramate). Classification: barbiturates (phenobarbitone, primidone), hydantoins (phenytoin, fosphenytoin), iminostilbenes (carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, eslicarbazepine), succinimides (ethosuximide), aliphatic carboxylic acid (valproate, divalproex), benzodiazepines (clonazepam, clobazam, diazepam, lorazepam, midazolam), phenyltriazine (lamotrigine), cyclic GABA analogues (gabapentin, pregabalin), newer drugs (levetiracetam, brivaracetam, topiramate, zonisamide, lacosamide, perampanel, rufinamide, vigabatrin, tiagabine, felbamate, cenobamate). Phenytoin: mechanism — use-dependent block of Na⁺ channels, prolonging inactivated state and preventing high-frequency repetitive firing; pharmacokinetics — slow erratic oral absorption, 90% protein bound, metabolised by CYP2C9/2C19 with saturation (Michaelis–Menten, zero-order) kinetics at therapeutic concentrations (10–20 µg/mL), so small dose increments cause large rises — therapeutic drug monitoring needed; potent enzyme inducer (reduces OCP, warfarin, steroids); interactions — levels raised by isoniazid, chloramphenicol, valproate (displacement), cimetidine; uses — generalised tonic–clonic and focal seizures, status epilepticus (IV or fosphenytoin), prophylaxis after neurosurgery/head injury, trigeminal neuralgia (second-line), class IB antiarrhythmic (digitalis toxicity, historical); adverse effects — dose-related: nystagmus, ataxia, diplopia, vertigo, slurred speech, drowsiness, confusion; chronic: gingival hyperplasia, hirsutism, coarsening of facial features, acne, megaloblastic anaemia (folate deficiency), osteomalacia (vitamin D metabolism), hyperglycaemia (inhibits insulin release), lymphadenopathy (pseudolymphoma); idiosyncratic: rashes, Stevens–Johnson syndrome/TEN (HLA-B*1502), hepatitis, DRESS, lupus; IV — hypotension, arrhythmias, purple glove syndrome; teratogenic — fetal hydantoin syndrome (cleft lip/palate, digital hypoplasia, microcephaly). Carbamazepine: mechanism — Na⁺ channel block; pharmacokinetics — slow absorption, autoinduction (t½ falls from 35 to 15 h after 2–4 weeks; dose titration), active epoxide metabolite, potent enzyme inducer; levels raised by erythromycin, cimetidine, valproate, isoniazid; uses — drug of choice for focal (partial) seizures with or without secondary generalisation, GTCS, trigeminal and glossopharyngeal neuralgia (first choice), bipolar disorder (mania prophylaxis), diabetes insipidus (central), neuropathic pain, alcohol withdrawal; ineffective/worsens absence and myoclonic seizures; adverse effects — dizziness, diplopia, ataxia, drowsiness, nausea, hyponatraemia (SIADH-like), rashes, Stevens–Johnson syndrome/TEN (strongly associated with HLA-B*1502 in Asians — screen before use), agranulocytosis, aplastic anaemia, leucopenia, hepatitis, teratogenicity (neural tube defects, craniofacial anomalies), weight gain, osteomalacia. Sodium valproate: mechanisms — Na⁺ channel block, T-type Ca²⁺ channel inhibition, increased GABA levels (inhibits GABA transaminase and succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase, stimulates GAD); broad-spectrum; pharmacokinetics — well absorbed, highly protein bound (saturable), metabolised in liver, enzyme inhibitor (raises phenobarbitone, lamotrigine, phenytoin free levels); uses — absence seizures (with or instead of ethosuximide; when GTCS coexist), myoclonic, atonic and generalised tonic–clonic seizures (drug of choice in idiopathic generalised epilepsy, juvenile myoclonic epilepsy), focal seizures, infantile spasms, Lennox–Gastaut, status epilepticus (IV), bipolar disorder (acute mania and prophylaxis), migraine prophylaxis; adverse effects — GI upset (anorexia, nausea), weight gain, tremor, alopecia (curly regrowth), drowsiness, thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction, hyperammonaemia (encephalopathy), fulminant hepatotoxicity (especially children < 2 years on polytherapy, metabolic disorders), acute pancreatitis, PCOS and menstrual irregularity, insulin resistance, teratogenicity — highest among AEDs (neural tube defects/spina bifida, cardiac and craniofacial defects, reduced IQ and autism) — avoid in women of childbearing potential unless no alternative (pregnancy prevention programme). General principles: monotherapy preferred; select drug by seizure type and patient factors; titrate slowly; avoid abrupt withdrawal; folic acid for women; therapeutic drug monitoring for phenytoin; newer drugs (levetiracetam, lamotrigine) favoured for better tolerability and fewer interactions.",
[["Drug choice by seizure type",[["Seizure","First choice","Alternatives"],["Focal ± secondary generalisation","Carbamazepine, lamotrigine, levetiracetam","Oxcarbazepine, phenytoin, valproate, lacosamide"],["Generalised tonic–clonic","Valproate","Lamotrigine, levetiracetam, carbamazepine, phenytoin"],["Absence","Ethosuximide, valproate","Lamotrigine"],["Myoclonic","Valproate","Levetiracetam, clonazepam, topiramate"],["Infantile spasms","ACTH, vigabatrin","Valproate"],["Status epilepticus","Lorazepam/diazepam IV → fosphenytoin/valproate/levetiracetam","Phenobarbitone, anaesthetics"]]],
["Comparison",[["Feature","Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproate"],["Mechanism","Na⁺ channel block","Na⁺ channel block","Na⁺, T-Ca²⁺, ↑ GABA"],["Kinetics","Zero-order; TDM","Autoinduction","Saturable binding"],["Enzymes","Inducer","Inducer","Inhibitor"],["Absence seizures","Ineffective","Worsens","Effective"],["Key ADRs","Gum hyperplasia, hirsutism, ataxia, osteomalacia","Hyponatraemia, SJS (HLA-B*1502), aplastic anaemia","Hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis, weight gain, tremor"],["Teratogenicity","Fetal hydantoin syndrome","NTD","NTD (highest), ↓ IQ"],["Other uses","Status epilepticus","Trigeminal neuralgia, bipolar","Bipolar, migraine"]]],
["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Hydantoins","Phenytoin, fosphenytoin"],["Barbiturates","Phenobarbitone, primidone"],["Iminostilbenes","Carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine"],["Succinimides","Ethosuximide"],["Carboxylic acid","Valproate"],["Benzodiazepines","Clonazepam, clobazam, diazepam, lorazepam"],["Newer","Lamotrigine, levetiracetam, topiramate, gabapentin, pregabalin, lacosamide, vigabatrin"]]]],
{i:"Epilepsy affects about 1% of people; antiepileptic drugs control seizures in about 70% with appropriate choice based on seizure type.",f:"Neuronal hyperexcitability\n ← Na⁺ channel block: phenytoin, carbamazepine, lamotrigine, valproate\n ← ↑ GABA: BZDs, barbiturates, valproate, vigabatrin\n ← T-type Ca²⁺ block: ethosuximide, valproate (absence)\n ← SV2A: levetiracetam; α2δ: gabapentin",c:"Antiepileptic therapy is individualised by seizure type, side-effect profile, interactions and reproductive considerations; phenytoin, carbamazepine and valproate remain key drugs, with newer agents offering better tolerability."});

Z("pharm6",4,"ph",["Status epilepticus is a seizure lasting ≥ 5 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery in between; it is an emergency treated with IV benzodiazepines first, then second-line antiseizure drugs and anaesthesia if needed.","Every minute counts — brain damage risk rises; stop the fire quickly with benzodiazepines, then a longer-acting drug to keep it off.",["0–5 min: ABC, oxygen, IV access, glucose check (give 50% dextrose + thiamine if hypoglycaemic).","5–20 min (first line): lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg IV (4 mg) or diazepam 10 mg IV/rectal, or midazolam 10 mg IM/buccal; repeat once.","20–40 min (second line): fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg or phenytoin 20 mg/kg IV (≤ 50 mg/min, ECG monitoring), or valproate 40 mg/kg, or levetiracetam 60 mg/kg, or phenobarbitone 20 mg/kg.","Refractory (> 40 min): ICU, intubation, anaesthetic infusion — midazolam, propofol, thiopentone with EEG monitoring; treat cause (infection, metabolic, drugs, eclampsia — MgSO4)."],"Status epilepticus: 'Benzo → Phenytoin/Valproate/Levetiracetam → Anaesthesia'."],
"Status epilepticus is a medical emergency defined as continuous seizure activity for 5 minutes or more (operational definition for convulsive status) or two or more seizures without full recovery of consciousness between them; prolonged seizures (> 30 minutes) cause neuronal injury. Causes: non-compliance or withdrawal of antiepileptic drugs, alcohol withdrawal, CNS infections (meningitis, encephalitis, neurocysticercosis), stroke, head injury, tumours, metabolic disturbances (hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, hypocalcaemia), drug toxicity, eclampsia, hypoxia. Management (staged protocol): stabilisation phase (0–5 min) — airway, breathing, circulation, oxygen, positioning, monitor vitals and ECG, time seizure, IV access, blood glucose (if < 60 mg/dL give 50 mL of 50% dextrose IV with thiamine 100 mg in adults), blood for electrolytes, calcium, magnesium, AED levels, toxicology, ABG; initial therapy (5–20 min) — benzodiazepine: lorazepam IV 0.1 mg/kg (max 4 mg per dose, repeat once) preferred (longer CNS action), or diazepam IV 0.15–0.2 mg/kg (max 10 mg), or midazolam IM 10 mg (> 40 kg) / buccal or intranasal; rectal diazepam in children without IV access; second therapy (20–40 min) if seizures continue — fosphenytoin 20 mg PE/kg IV (up to 150 mg PE/min) or phenytoin 20 mg/kg IV in saline (not dextrose; ≤ 50 mg/min; monitor ECG and BP for arrhythmia and hypotension), or sodium valproate 40 mg/kg IV (max 3000 mg), or levetiracetam 60 mg/kg IV (max 4500 mg), or phenobarbitone 15–20 mg/kg IV (if others unavailable; respiratory depression); third phase — refractory status (> 40 min, failure of two drugs): ICU admission, endotracheal intubation and ventilation, continuous EEG monitoring, anaesthetic doses of midazolam infusion, propofol, thiopentone or pentobarbitone to achieve burst suppression; super-refractory — ketamine, inhalational anaesthetics, immunotherapy. Throughout: identify and treat the cause (antibiotics/antivirals for infection, correct electrolytes, magnesium sulphate for eclampsia, pyridoxine for INH toxicity), manage hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, aspiration and cerebral oedema; restart maintenance AEDs.",
[["Protocol",[["Time","Phase","Drugs"],["0–5 min","Stabilisation","ABC, O2, glucose (+ thiamine)"],["5–20 min","Initial","Lorazepam IV / diazepam IV / midazolam IM"],["20–40 min","Second line","Fosphenytoin/phenytoin, valproate, levetiracetam, phenobarbitone"],["> 40 min","Refractory","Midazolam/propofol/thiopentone infusion, ICU, EEG"]]]],
{c:"Status epilepticus requires rapid, protocol-driven therapy: IV benzodiazepines first, a second-line antiseizure drug next, and anaesthetic infusion with ventilatory support for refractory cases, while treating the cause."});

Z("pharm6",5,"ph",["Parkinsonism results from dopamine loss in the basal ganglia; drugs either replace dopamine (levodopa with carbidopa), mimic it (dopamine agonists), prevent its breakdown (MAO-B, COMT inhibitors) or reduce cholinergic overactivity (anticholinergics).","Dopamine and acetylcholine are on a seesaw; in Parkinson's dopamine falls, so we add dopamine (levodopa) or reduce acetylcholine (anticholinergics).",["Classification: dopamine precursor (levodopa), peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors (carbidopa, benserazide), dopamine agonists (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine, bromocriptine, apomorphine), MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline, safinamide), COMT inhibitors (entacapone, opicapone, tolcapone), amantadine, anticholinergics (trihexyphenidyl/benzhexol, benztropine).","Levodopa: crosses BBB (LAT carrier), decarboxylated to dopamine; most effective drug for bradykinesia and rigidity.","Levodopa + carbidopa: carbidopa (doesn't cross BBB) blocks peripheral decarboxylation → ↓ levodopa dose by 75%, ↓ nausea/vomiting, arrhythmia, more brain delivery; pyridoxine no longer reverses effect.","ADR: nausea, postural hypotension, arrhythmias, dyskinesias, psychosis, hallucinations, impulse control; on-off phenomenon (sudden fluctuations — 'wearing off'), end-of-dose deterioration; managed by frequent smaller doses, COMT/MAO-B inhibitors, agonists, controlled-release, apomorphine."],"Carbidopa = 'Car stays outside (periphery)', lets levodopa drive into brain."],
"Parkinsonism is a syndrome of bradykinesia, rigidity, rest tremor and postural instability due to degeneration of dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta projecting to the striatum, resulting in dopamine deficiency and relative cholinergic overactivity. Classification of antiparkinsonian drugs: (I) drugs affecting brain dopaminergic system — (a) dopamine precursor: levodopa; (b) peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors: carbidopa, benserazide; (c) dopaminergic agonists: ergot — bromocriptine, cabergoline (rarely now); non-ergot — ropinirole, pramipexole, rotigotine (patch), apomorphine (SC rescue); (d) MAO-B inhibitors: selegiline, rasagiline, safinamide; (e) COMT inhibitors: entacapone, opicapone, tolcapone; (f) dopamine facilitator: amantadine (also NMDA antagonist — anti-dyskinetic); (II) drugs affecting brain cholinergic system — central anticholinergics: trihexyphenidyl (benzhexol), procyclidine, biperiden, benztropine; antihistamines with anticholinergic property (orphenadrine, promethazine). Levodopa: dopamine does not cross the BBB, but its precursor levodopa does via the L-type neutral amino acid transporter; in the brain it is decarboxylated by dopa decarboxylase to dopamine in remaining nigrostriatal neurons. Pharmacokinetics: absorbed from small intestine by active transport (competes with dietary proteins), > 95% decarboxylated peripherally in gut wall, liver and blood vessels if given alone (only 1–2% reaches brain), t½ 1–2 h. Actions: improves bradykinesia and rigidity dramatically, tremor less; peripheral dopamine causes nausea and vomiting (CTZ outside BBB), tachycardia and arrhythmias, postural hypotension; endocrine — inhibits prolactin, increases GH. Uses: Parkinson disease (most effective drug; gold standard), restless legs syndrome; not useful in drug-induced parkinsonism (dopamine receptors blocked). Adverse effects: early — nausea, vomiting, anorexia, postural hypotension, cardiac arrhythmias, exacerbation of angina, alterations in taste; late (after years) — motor fluctuations (wearing-off/end-of-dose deterioration; on–off phenomenon), dyskinesias (choreoathetoid involuntary movements, peak-dose), behavioural effects (confusion, vivid dreams, hallucinations, psychosis, depression, impulse control disorders, dopamine dysregulation syndrome), mydriasis (worsens angle-closure glaucoma), urine and sweat darkening. Interactions: pyridoxine (enhances peripheral decarboxylation when levodopa alone), non-selective MAO inhibitors (hypertensive crisis), antipsychotics and metoclopramide (block D2 — antagonise), antimuscarinics (delay gastric emptying, reducing absorption), high-protein meals. Levodopa + carbidopa (or benserazide): carbidopa is a peripheral dopa decarboxylase inhibitor that does not cross the BBB; it prevents peripheral conversion, so (i) plasma t½ and brain availability of levodopa increase, (ii) dose of levodopa can be reduced by ~75%, (iii) peripheral adverse effects — nausea, vomiting, cardiac arrhythmias — are markedly reduced, (iv) response is smoother and earlier, (v) pyridoxine interaction is abolished; ratio 1:4 or 1:10 (e.g., 25/100, 10/100); dyskinesias and psychiatric effects are not reduced (may be more prominent). On–off effect: abrupt unpredictable fluctuations in motor function in long-term levodopa therapy, switching within minutes from mobility ('on', often with dyskinesia) to severe akinesia ('off'), unrelated to dose timing; attributed to progressive loss of presynaptic dopamine storage capacity and pulsatile receptor stimulation; wearing-off is predictable end-of-dose deterioration. Management of fluctuations: smaller, more frequent doses, controlled-release levodopa, adding COMT inhibitor (entacapone) or MAO-B inhibitor (rasagiline) to prolong action, dopamine agonists, avoiding protein meals near doses, SC apomorphine for 'off' episodes, levodopa–carbidopa intestinal gel, deep brain stimulation; amantadine for dyskinesias; drug holiday (not recommended).",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Dopamine precursor","Levodopa"],["Peripheral DDC inhibitors","Carbidopa, benserazide"],["Dopamine agonists","Pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine, apomorphine, bromocriptine"],["MAO-B inhibitors","Selegiline, rasagiline, safinamide"],["COMT inhibitors","Entacapone, opicapone, tolcapone"],["Others","Amantadine"],["Anticholinergics","Trihexyphenidyl, benztropine, procyclidine"]]],
["Levodopa alone vs with carbidopa",[["Feature","Levodopa alone","Levodopa + carbidopa"],["Brain delivery","1–2%","~10%"],["Dose","High","↓ 75%"],["Nausea, vomiting","Marked","Less"],["Cardiac arrhythmias","More","Less"],["Pyridoxine interaction","Present","Abolished"],["Dyskinesia, psychosis","Present","Present (may increase)"]]],
["Adverse effects of levodopa",["Nausea, vomiting, anorexia","Postural hypotension, arrhythmias","Dyskinesias","Motor fluctuations: wearing-off, on–off","Hallucinations, psychosis, impulse control disorders","Darkening of urine"]]],
{i:"Levodopa remains the most effective symptomatic treatment of Parkinson disease more than 50 years after its introduction.",x:[["On–off phenomenon",["Sudden unpredictable switching between mobility and akinesia","Seen after 3–5 years of levodopa","Management: frequent small doses, CR preparations, COMT/MAO-B inhibitors, agonists, apomorphine rescue, intestinal gel, DBS"]]],f:"Levodopa (oral) → gut/blood: peripheral DDC → dopamine (nausea, arrhythmia) ← blocked by carbidopa\n → LAT across BBB → striatal DDC → dopamine → D1/D2 → ↓ bradykinesia, rigidity\n Breakdown: MAO-B (selegiline blocks), COMT (entacapone blocks)",c:"Levodopa with carbidopa is the cornerstone of Parkinson disease therapy; adjunctive agonists, MAO-B and COMT inhibitors and other measures manage the motor fluctuations that emerge with long-term use."});

Z("pharm6",6,"ph",["Antipsychotics (neuroleptics) treat psychosis such as schizophrenia by blocking dopamine D2 receptors (typical) or D2 plus serotonin 5-HT2A receptors (atypical).","They turn down an overactive dopamine signal in the brain's mesolimbic pathway, quietening hallucinations and delusions; blocking dopamine elsewhere causes movement and hormonal side effects.",["Typical: phenothiazines (chlorpromazine, thioridazine, fluphenazine, trifluoperazine), butyrophenones (haloperidol), thioxanthenes (flupenthixol), others (pimozide, loxapine).","Atypical: clozapine, risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, aripiprazole (partial D2 agonist), amisulpride, lurasidone.","Uses: schizophrenia, acute mania and bipolar disorder, delusional and organic psychoses, agitation, nausea/vomiting (prochlorperazine, haloperidol), hiccups, Tourette's, augmentation in depression.","ADR: extrapyramidal (acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia), neuroleptic malignant syndrome, hyperprolactinaemia, anticholinergic, postural hypotension (α block), sedation (H1), weight gain/diabetes/dyslipidaemia (atypicals), QT prolongation, agranulocytosis (clozapine), lowered seizure threshold."],"Typicals → EPS; atypicals → metabolic syndrome."],
"Antipsychotic (neuroleptic) drugs are used to treat psychoses, chiefly schizophrenia, and also mania. Classification: (I) typical (first-generation) — phenothiazines: aliphatic (chlorpromazine, triflupromazine), piperidine (thioridazine), piperazine (trifluoperazine, fluphenazine, prochlorperazine); butyrophenones (haloperidol, droperidol, trifluperidol); thioxanthenes (flupenthixol, zuclopenthixol); others (pimozide, loxapine, penfluridol); depot preparations (fluphenazine decanoate, haloperidol decanoate, flupenthixol decanoate); (II) atypical (second-generation) — clozapine, risperidone, paliperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine (D2/D3 partial agonists), amisulpride, sulpiride, lurasidone, asenapine, iloperidone; depot — risperidone, paliperidone, aripiprazole long-acting injections. Mechanism: antipsychotic efficacy correlates with blockade of dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways (dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia); blockade in nigrostriatal pathway causes extrapyramidal symptoms, in tuberoinfundibular pathway hyperprolactinaemia, and in CTZ antiemetic effect; atypical agents have higher 5-HT2A:D2 blockade ratio (serotonin–dopamine antagonists), faster dissociation from D2, or partial agonism, resulting in fewer EPS and possible benefit on negative symptoms; other receptor blockades — muscarinic (dry mouth, constipation), α1-adrenergic (postural hypotension, impotence), H1 (sedation, weight gain), 5-HT2C (weight gain). Actions: neuroleptic syndrome — emotional quietening, psychomotor slowing, indifference; reduction of hallucinations, delusions, thought disorder (positive symptoms); antiemetic; lowering of seizure threshold; poikilothermia; endocrine — galactorrhoea, gynaecomastia, amenorrhoea. Uses: schizophrenia (acute and maintenance; clozapine for treatment-resistant), acute mania and bipolar disorder (olanzapine, quetiapine, risperidone, aripiprazole), psychotic depression (with antidepressant), organic brain syndromes and delirium (haloperidol low dose), agitation and aggression, Tourette syndrome and Huntington chorea (haloperidol, tetrabenazine), nausea and vomiting (prochlorperazine, chlorpromazine, haloperidol, droperidol), intractable hiccups (chlorpromazine), neuroleptanalgesia (droperidol + fentanyl), autism-related irritability (risperidone, aripiprazole), augmentation in depression, alcohol withdrawal agitation, pruritus (trimeprazine). Adverse effects: (1) extrapyramidal — acute dystonia (within hours–days; torticollis, oculogyric crisis; treat with IV anticholinergic/promethazine), akathisia (restlessness; propranolol, benzodiazepine), drug-induced parkinsonism (weeks; anticholinergics), tardive dyskinesia (months–years; orofacial involuntary movements; often irreversible; worsened by anticholinergics; valbenazine/tetrabenazine, switch to clozapine), perioral tremor (rabbit syndrome); (2) neuroleptic malignant syndrome — rigidity, hyperthermia, autonomic instability, altered consciousness, raised CPK, myoglobinuria; stop drug, cooling, dantrolene, bromocriptine; (3) CNS — sedation, seizures, confusion; (4) anticholinergic — dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, urinary retention; (5) cardiovascular — postural hypotension, tachycardia, QT prolongation and torsades (thioridazine, pimozide, ziprasidone, IV haloperidol), sudden death, myocarditis (clozapine); (6) endocrine — hyperprolactinaemia (galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, gynaecomastia, sexual dysfunction — risperidone, typicals; spared by aripiprazole, quetiapine, clozapine); (7) metabolic — weight gain, insulin resistance, diabetes, dyslipidaemia (clozapine, olanzapine most); (8) haematological — agranulocytosis (clozapine), leucopenia; (9) hepatic — cholestatic jaundice (chlorpromazine); (10) skin and eye — photosensitivity, pigmentation, corneal and lens deposits (chlorpromazine), pigmentary retinopathy (thioridazine); (11) increased mortality in elderly patients with dementia (stroke). Interactions: potentiate CNS depressants, antagonise levodopa, additive QT prolongation.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Phenothiazines","Chlorpromazine, thioridazine, fluphenazine, trifluoperazine, prochlorperazine"],["Butyrophenones","Haloperidol, droperidol"],["Thioxanthenes","Flupenthixol"],["Atypical","Clozapine, risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, aripiprazole, amisulpride, lurasidone"],["Depot","Fluphenazine/haloperidol decanoate, paliperidone palmitate"]]],
["Typical vs atypical",[["Feature","Typical","Atypical"],["Receptor","D2 blockade","5-HT2A + D2 (or partial agonism)"],["Positive symptoms","Effective","Effective"],["Negative symptoms","Less effective","More effective"],["EPS","Common","Less"],["Prolactin rise","Common","Less (except risperidone, amisulpride)"],["Metabolic effects","Less","Weight gain, diabetes, dyslipidaemia"],["Treatment-resistant","—","Clozapine"]]],
["Extrapyramidal side effects",[["EPS","Onset","Treatment"],["Acute dystonia","Hours–days","IV anticholinergic/antihistamine"],["Akathisia","Days–weeks","Propranolol, BZD, ↓ dose"],["Parkinsonism","Weeks","Anticholinergic (benzhexol)"],["Tardive dyskinesia","Months–years","Stop/switch, VMAT2 inhibitors"],["NMS","Any time","Stop drug, dantrolene, bromocriptine"]]]],
{i:"Antipsychotics transformed the management of schizophrenia; their efficacy relates to D2 blockade, while differences in other receptor actions determine side-effect profiles.",f:"Dopamine pathways blocked by antipsychotics:\n Mesolimbic → ↓ positive symptoms (therapeutic)\n Mesocortical → negative/cognitive (worsened by typicals)\n Nigrostriatal → EPS\n Tuberoinfundibular → ↑ prolactin\n CTZ → antiemetic",c:"Antipsychotics control psychosis through dopamine blockade; choosing between typical and atypical agents balances extrapyramidal against metabolic risks, with clozapine reserved for resistant cases."});

Z("pharm6",7,"ph",["Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic, the most effective for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and suicidal behaviour, but it can cause agranulocytosis, so blood counts must be monitored.","The 'last-resort but best' antipsychotic: powerful where others fail, almost no movement side effects, but needs regular blood count checks.",["Weak D2, strong D4, 5-HT2A, α1, H1, muscarinic blockade; minimal EPS and prolactin rise; no tardive dyskinesia (may improve it).","Uses: treatment-resistant schizophrenia (failure of 2 antipsychotics), suicidal schizophrenia, psychosis in Parkinson's disease, tardive dyskinesia.","ADR: agranulocytosis (~1%; monitor ANC weekly 18 weeks, then monthly), seizures (dose-related), myocarditis/cardiomyopathy, sialorrhoea (hypersalivation), weight gain, diabetes, dyslipidaemia, sedation, constipation/ileus, orthostatic hypotension, fever.","Interactions: levels ↑ by fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin; ↓ by smoking (CYP1A2 inducer)."],"Clozapine risks: 'ANC drop, seizures, myocarditis, drooling, weight gain'."],
"Clozapine is the prototype atypical antipsychotic (dibenzodiazepine). Receptor profile: relatively weak D2 antagonist (low occupancy, rapid dissociation), higher affinity for D4, potent 5-HT2A and 5-HT2C antagonist, 5-HT1A partial agonist, strong α1 and α2 adrenergic, H1 histaminergic and M1 muscarinic antagonist (but M4 agonism — hypersalivation). Consequently it produces virtually no extrapyramidal side effects or tardive dyskinesia (may improve TD), little prolactin elevation, and is effective for both positive and negative symptoms. Efficacy: the only antipsychotic proven superior in treatment-resistant schizophrenia (failure of adequate trials of two different antipsychotics; ~30–60% respond) and in reducing suicidal behaviour in schizophrenia/schizoaffective disorder. Uses: treatment-resistant schizophrenia, schizophrenia with persistent suicidality, psychosis in Parkinson disease (low dose; does not worsen motor symptoms), severe tardive dyskinesia, schizoaffective and refractory bipolar disorder. Pharmacokinetics: oral; metabolised by CYP1A2 (and 3A4) to norclozapine; t½ 12 h; levels reduced by smoking (induction — levels rise on stopping smoking), increased by fluvoxamine, ciprofloxacin, erythromycin; plasma level monitoring (> 350 ng/mL target). Adverse effects: agranulocytosis/severe neutropenia (about 0.8%; mostly in first 18 weeks; immune-mediated) — mandatory monitoring of absolute neutrophil count before starting, weekly for 18 weeks, then fortnightly/monthly; stop if ANC < 1000/µL; avoid other marrow-suppressing drugs (carbamazepine); seizures (dose-dependent, up to 5% at > 600 mg/day — consider valproate); myocarditis (first weeks) and cardiomyopathy — monitor troponin, CRP, ECG; orthostatic hypotension and tachycardia (slow titration), syncope; sedation; hypersalivation (nocturnal drooling); weight gain, diabetes, dyslipidaemia (metabolic syndrome; highest risk with olanzapine); constipation, paralytic ileus (potentially fatal — clozapine-induced gastrointestinal hypomotility); fever (benign early), urinary incontinence, NMS (rare), pulmonary embolism, hepatitis.",
[["Monitoring",["ANC before start; weekly × 18 weeks; then 2–4 weekly","Troponin, CRP, ECG for myocarditis (first 4 weeks)","Weight, glucose, lipids","Bowel habits (constipation)","Plasma level; smoking status"]],
["Receptor profile",[["Receptor","Effect"],["Weak D2","Minimal EPS, prolactin"],["5-HT2A block","Antipsychotic, ↓ EPS"],["H1, 5-HT2C block","Sedation, weight gain"],["α1 block","Hypotension"],["Muscarinic","Constipation; M4 agonism — drooling"]]]],
{c:"Clozapine is uniquely effective for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and suicidality, but requires strict haematological, cardiac and metabolic monitoring."});

Z("pharm6",8,"ph",["Lithium is a mood stabiliser for bipolar disorder that prevents manic and depressive episodes; it has a narrow therapeutic range, so blood levels must be monitored.","Lithium works like a thermostat for mood, stopping it swinging too high (mania) or too low (depression), but the dose window between benefit and poison is very narrow.",["Mechanism: inhibits inositol monophosphatase (inositol depletion hypothesis) and GSK-3β; ↓ NA/DA release, ↑ 5-HT.","Uses: acute mania (with antipsychotic), prophylaxis of bipolar disorder (first-line), recurrent unipolar depression augmentation, suicide prevention, SIADH (rarely).","Therapeutic level: 0.6–1.0 mEq/L (prophylaxis), 0.8–1.2 (acute mania); toxicity > 1.5.","ADR: tremor, polyuria (nephrogenic DI), hypothyroidism, weight gain, acne, leucocytosis, Ebstein anomaly (teratogen), toxicity — vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion, seizures; interactions — thiazides, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, dehydration ↑ levels."],"Lithium side effects 'LITHIUM': Leucocytosis, Insipidus (DI), Tremor, Hypothyroidism, Increased weight, Upset stomach, Malformation (Ebstein)."],
"Lithium carbonate is the classical mood stabiliser for bipolar affective disorder. Mechanism (not fully understood): lithium substitutes for Na⁺ and K⁺ in cells; it inhibits inositol monophosphatase and other phosphatases, depleting free inositol and dampening phosphatidylinositol (IP3/DAG) second messenger signalling in overactive neurons (inositol depletion hypothesis); inhibits glycogen synthase kinase-3β (neuroprotective, neurotrophic); reduces release of noradrenaline and dopamine, increases serotonergic transmission; inhibits adenylyl cyclase coupling (ADH and TSH actions). Pharmacokinetics: completely absorbed orally, not protein bound, slow entry into brain, not metabolised, excreted unchanged by kidney (80% reabsorbed in proximal tubule with Na⁺ — sodium depletion increases lithium reabsorption and toxicity); t½ 20–24 h; narrow therapeutic index — serum level monitoring 12 hours after last dose: 0.6–1.0 mEq/L for prophylaxis, 0.8–1.2 mEq/L for acute mania; toxicity > 1.5 mEq/L, severe > 2.5. Uses: bipolar disorder — prophylaxis of manic and depressive episodes (first-line; reduces suicide risk), acute mania (slow onset 1–2 weeks; combined with antipsychotics or benzodiazepines initially), bipolar depression; augmentation of antidepressants in resistant unipolar depression; recurrent depression prophylaxis; schizoaffective disorder; aggression; cluster headache prophylaxis; neutropenia (stimulates granulopoiesis); SIADH (largely replaced). Adverse effects: early — nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, fine tremor (propranolol), polyuria and polydipsia, muscle weakness, metallic taste; long-term — nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (blocks ADH action — amiloride helps), chronic tubulointerstitial nephropathy and CKD, hypothyroidism and goitre (inhibits hormone release), hyperparathyroidism and hypercalcaemia, weight gain, oedema, acne, psoriasis exacerbation, alopecia, benign leucocytosis, ECG T-wave flattening, sinus node dysfunction; teratogenicity — Ebstein anomaly of tricuspid valve (first trimester); neonatal toxicity, excreted in milk. Toxicity: vomiting, profuse diarrhoea, coarse tremor, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus, confusion, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, seizures, coma, arrhythmias, renal failure; precipitated by dehydration, salt restriction, diuretics, intercurrent illness; treatment — stop lithium, IV saline, correct electrolytes, haemodialysis for severe toxicity (no role for charcoal); whole bowel irrigation for sustained-release overdose. Interactions: thiazide diuretics, loop diuretics (less), ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs (except aspirin), metronidazole raise lithium levels; theophylline, caffeine, acetazolamide, osmotic diuretics increase excretion; antipsychotics (neurotoxicity), SSRIs (serotonin syndrome). Monitoring: baseline and periodic renal function, thyroid function, calcium, ECG (> 40 years), pregnancy test; serum lithium every 3 months once stable. Alternatives: valproate, carbamazepine, lamotrigine (bipolar depression), atypical antipsychotics.",
[["Therapeutic levels",[["Indication","Serum Li⁺ (mEq/L)"],["Prophylaxis","0.6–1.0"],["Acute mania","0.8–1.2"],["Toxic","> 1.5"],["Severe toxicity","> 2.5"]]],
["Adverse effects",["Tremor, GI upset","Polyuria (nephrogenic DI)","Hypothyroidism, goitre","Weight gain, oedema, acne","Leucocytosis","Renal impairment (long-term)","Ebstein anomaly (teratogen)"]],
["Drugs increasing lithium levels",["Thiazide diuretics","ACE inhibitors, ARBs","NSAIDs","Dehydration, low salt diet","Metronidazole"]]],
{c:"Lithium is a first-line mood stabiliser that reduces relapses and suicide in bipolar disorder; its narrow therapeutic index demands careful level, renal and thyroid monitoring and attention to interactions."});

Z("pharm6",9,"ph",["Antidepressants treat depression mainly by increasing serotonin and/or noradrenaline in the brain; tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) are older and have many side effects, while SSRIs are newer, safer first-line drugs.","Both increase mood messengers by blocking their 'vacuum cleaners' (reuptake pumps); TCAs also block many other receptors (more side effects), SSRIs block only the serotonin pump.",["TCAs: imipramine, amitriptyline, nortriptyline, clomipramine, desipramine, dothiepin; block NET and SERT; also block M, α1, H1, Na⁺ channels.","TCA ADR: anticholinergic (dry mouth, constipation, retention), postural hypotension, sedation, weight gain, cardiac arrhythmias (QRS widening), seizures; overdose dangerous (3 Cs: coma, convulsions, cardiotoxicity — treat with sodium bicarbonate).","SSRIs: fluoxetine, sertraline, paroxetine, citalopram, escitalopram, fluvoxamine; first-line for depression, anxiety, OCD, PTSD, panic, bulimia, PMDD.","SSRI ADR: nausea, sexual dysfunction, insomnia/anxiety initially, headache, hyponatraemia (elderly), bleeding (with NSAIDs), discontinuation syndrome (paroxetine), suicidal thoughts in young (< 25), serotonin syndrome with MAOIs/tramadol/triptans; safer in overdose. Onset 2–4 weeks."],"TCA overdose '3 Cs': Coma, Convulsions, Cardiotoxicity → NaHCO3."],
"Antidepressants are drugs that elevate mood in depressive illness. Classification: (1) tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs; reuptake inhibitors of NA and 5-HT) — imipramine, amitriptyline, trimipramine, doxepin, dothiepin (dosulepin), clomipramine (more 5-HT; OCD), nortriptyline, desipramine (more NA), protriptyline, amoxapine; (2) selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) — fluoxetine, sertraline, paroxetine, citalopram, escitalopram, fluvoxamine, dapoxetine (premature ejaculation); (3) serotonin–noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) — venlafaxine, desvenlafaxine, duloxetine, milnacipran; (4) atypical antidepressants — bupropion (NDRI), mirtazapine (α2 antagonist, NaSSA), trazodone, nefazodone (SARIs), tianeptine, vortioxetine, vilazodone, agomelatine (MT1/MT2 agonist, 5-HT2C antagonist); (5) MAO inhibitors — non-selective irreversible (phenelzine, tranylcypromine), reversible MAO-A (moclobemide); (6) newer — esketamine (NMDA antagonist; nasal), brexanolone/zuranolone (postpartum). TCAs: mechanism — inhibit neuronal reuptake of noradrenaline (NET) and serotonin (SERT), increasing synaptic concentrations; adaptive changes (downregulation of β-adrenergic and 5-HT2 receptors, increased BDNF) over 2–4 weeks correlate with therapeutic effect; also block muscarinic, α1-adrenergic, H1 histamine receptors and fast Na⁺ channels (quinidine-like). Pharmacokinetics: well absorbed, high first-pass, highly protein bound and lipophilic (large Vd — not dialysable), metabolised by CYP2D6 to active metabolites (imipramine → desipramine, amitriptyline → nortriptyline), long t½ (once-daily bedtime dosing). Uses: major depression (now second-line), neuropathic pain and chronic pain syndromes (amitriptyline, nortriptyline), migraine prophylaxis, nocturnal enuresis in children (imipramine), obsessive–compulsive disorder (clomipramine), panic disorder, ADHD (desipramine), insomnia (low-dose doxepin). Adverse effects: anticholinergic — dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (BPH), blurred vision, precipitation of angle-closure glaucoma, confusion in elderly; α1 block — postural hypotension; H1 block — sedation, weight gain; cardiac — tachycardia, prolonged PR, QRS and QT, arrhythmias, sudden death (avoid after MI); lower seizure threshold; tremor; sexual dysfunction; mania switch; withdrawal symptoms. Acute TCA poisoning (narrow safety margin; 10–20× daily dose may be fatal): anticholinergic toxidrome, coma, convulsions, respiratory depression, hypotension, wide QRS > 100 ms, ventricular arrhythmias; treatment — activated charcoal, supportive care, IV sodium bicarbonate (alkalinisation and Na⁺ load overcome Na⁺ channel blockade; target pH 7.45–7.55), benzodiazepines for seizures, avoid physostigmine and class IA/IC antiarrhythmics, lipid emulsion in refractory cases. SSRIs: mechanism — selective inhibition of serotonin transporter, little affinity for other receptors; pharmacokinetics — fluoxetine has long t½ (2–4 days; active norfluoxetine 7–10 days — no withdrawal, long washout before MAOI); paroxetine and fluoxetine inhibit CYP2D6 (interactions with TCAs, tamoxifen, codeine), fluvoxamine inhibits CYP1A2; uses — first-line for major depressive disorder (including in elderly, cardiac patients — sertraline), dysthymia, generalised anxiety disorder, panic disorder, social anxiety, obsessive–compulsive disorder (higher doses), PTSD, bulimia nervosa (fluoxetine), premenstrual dysphoric disorder, premature ejaculation, hot flushes; adverse effects — nausea, dyspepsia, diarrhoea (early), headache, insomnia, nervousness, agitation, jitteriness at start, sweating, sexual dysfunction (anorgasmia, delayed ejaculation, reduced libido — common), weight changes, hyponatraemia/SIADH (elderly), bleeding risk (platelet serotonin depletion; GI bleed with NSAIDs, anticoagulants), extrapyramidal symptoms, apathy, QT prolongation (citalopram, escitalopram), increased suicidal thoughts in children, adolescents and young adults (black box warning — monitor), switch to mania in bipolar, discontinuation syndrome (dizziness, paraesthesia, irritability — especially paroxetine; taper), neonatal persistent pulmonary hypertension and withdrawal (late pregnancy), teratogenicity (paroxetine — cardiac defects); serotonin syndrome when combined with MAOIs, linezolid, tramadol, pethidine, triptans, St John's wort (agitation, hyperthermia, clonus, hyperreflexia, autonomic instability — stop drugs, benzodiazepines, cyproheptadine). Advantages over TCAs: better tolerated, no anticholinergic, cardiac or sedative effects, safe in overdose, once-daily dosing, better compliance.",
[["TCAs vs SSRIs",[["Feature","TCAs","SSRIs"],["Mechanism","Block NET + SERT; also M, α1, H1, Na⁺","Selective SERT block"],["Examples","Imipramine, amitriptyline","Fluoxetine, sertraline, escitalopram"],["Anticholinergic effects","Marked","Absent"],["Sedation, weight gain","Common","Less"],["Cardiotoxicity","Yes (QRS, arrhythmias)","Minimal (QT with citalopram)"],["Overdose","Dangerous","Relatively safe"],["Sexual dysfunction","Less","Common"],["Other uses","Neuropathic pain, enuresis, migraine","OCD, panic, PTSD, bulimia, PMDD"],["First-line for depression","No","Yes"]]],
["Serotonin syndrome",["Causes: SSRI + MAOI, linezolid, tramadol, triptans, pethidine","Features: agitation, hyperthermia, clonus, hyperreflexia, tachycardia, diarrhoea","Treatment: stop drugs, benzodiazepines, cyproheptadine, cooling"]]],
{i:"Depression is a leading cause of disability; antidepressants require 2–4 weeks for effect and should be continued 6–12 months after remission.",f:"Presynaptic neuron → NA/5-HT release → reuptake via NET/SERT\n TCA: blocks NET + SERT (+ M, α1, H1, Na⁺ → ADRs)\n SSRI: blocks SERT only\n → ↑ synaptic monoamines → receptor adaptation over 2–4 weeks → mood elevation",c:"SSRIs are first-line antidepressants because of their tolerability and safety in overdose; TCAs remain useful for neuropathic pain and selected patients but carry anticholinergic and cardiotoxic risks."});

Z("pharm6",10,"ph",["Opioid analgesics relieve severe pain by acting on opioid receptors (μ, κ, δ) in the brain and spinal cord; morphine is the prototype, and naloxone is the antidote that reverses opioid overdose.","Morphine turns up the body's natural painkilling system (endorphins); naloxone kicks morphine off the receptor instantly.",["Classification: natural (morphine, codeine, thebaine), semisynthetic (diacetylmorphine/heroin, hydromorphone, oxycodone), synthetic (pethidine, fentanyl, methadone, tramadol, tapentadol); agonist–antagonists (pentazocine, butorphanol, nalbuphine); partial agonist (buprenorphine); antagonists (naloxone, naltrexone, nalmefene).","Morphine MOA: μ-receptor agonist (Gi) → ↓ cAMP, opens K⁺ channels, closes Ca²⁺ channels → ↓ neurotransmitter release in pain pathways; activates descending inhibitory pathways.","Actions/uses: analgesia (cancer, MI, postoperative, trauma pain), sedation, euphoria; acute LVF (pulmonary oedema), preanaesthetic; cough (codeine); diarrhoea (loperamide).","ADR: respiratory depression (main cause of death), nausea/vomiting, constipation (no tolerance), miosis (pinpoint pupils; no tolerance), urinary retention, biliary spasm, hypotension, histamine release (itching, bronchospasm), tolerance, dependence; CI — head injury, asthma, COPD, hypothyroidism, infants. Naloxone: competitive antagonist, IV 0.4–2 mg, short t½ (30–60 min) — repeat/infusion; precipitates withdrawal."],"Morphine ADR 'MORPHINE': Miosis, Out of it (sedation), Respiratory depression, Pruritus, Hypotension, Itching/Inc ICP, Nausea, Emesis (& constipation)."],
"Opioid analgesics are drugs with morphine-like actions mediated by opioid receptors — μ (MOP; supraspinal and spinal analgesia, respiratory depression, euphoria, miosis, constipation, physical dependence), κ (KOP; spinal analgesia, sedation, dysphoria, diuresis, miosis), δ (DOP; analgesia, mood) and nociceptin (NOP) — G-protein coupled (Gi/o) receptors. Endogenous ligands: β-endorphin, enkephalins, dynorphins. Classification: (I) natural opium alkaloids — morphine, codeine (methylmorphine), thebaine; (II) semisynthetic — diacetylmorphine (heroin), pholcodine, ethylmorphine, hydromorphone, oxymorphone, oxycodone, hydrocodone, desomorphine; (III) synthetic — pethidine (meperidine), fentanyl, sufentanil, alfentanil, remifentanil, methadone, dextropropoxyphene, tramadol, tapentadol, levorphanol, diphenoxylate and loperamide (antidiarrhoeal); (IV) mixed agonist–antagonists and partial agonists — pentazocine, butorphanol, nalbuphine (κ agonist, μ antagonist), buprenorphine (partial μ agonist, κ antagonist); (V) pure antagonists — naloxone, naltrexone, nalmefene; peripherally acting — methylnaltrexone, naloxegol, alvimopan (opioid-induced constipation). Morphine: mechanism — agonist at μ (and κ, δ) receptors; via Gi proteins inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens K⁺ channels (postsynaptic hyperpolarisation) and closes voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels (reducing presynaptic release of glutamate, substance P, CGRP) in the dorsal horn, periaqueductal grey, rostral ventromedial medulla and thalamus; activates descending inhibitory serotonergic and noradrenergic pathways; alters affective component of pain (limbic). Pharmacological actions: CNS — analgesia (dull, continuous visceral pain better than sharp intermittent; raises pain threshold and reduces emotional response), sedation, euphoria (dysphoria in some), mental clouding, respiratory depression (reduced sensitivity of respiratory centre to CO2; dose-dependent; main cause of death in overdose), cough suppression (antitussive), miosis (stimulation of Edinger–Westphal nucleus; pinpoint pupils in poisoning; no tolerance), nausea and vomiting (CTZ stimulation), stimulation of vagal centre (bradycardia), hypothermia, release of ADH and prolactin, reduced LH/FSH, truncal rigidity (high-dose fentanyl), seizures at very high doses; CVS — vasodilatation, postural hypotension (histamine release, central), bradycardia; reduces preload and myocardial O2 demand; GIT — constipation (reduced propulsive peristalsis, increased tone, reduced secretions; no tolerance), increased biliary pressure (sphincter of Oddi spasm), delayed gastric emptying; urinary — retention (increased sphincter tone, reduced bladder awareness); bronchoconstriction (histamine release); skin — flushing, pruritus, urticaria; uterus — prolongs labour; immune suppression. Pharmacokinetics: oral bioavailability 20–40% (high first-pass); given IV, IM, SC, epidural, intrathecal, oral sustained-release; crosses placenta; metabolised in liver by glucuronidation to morphine-3-glucuronide (neuroexcitatory) and morphine-6-glucuronide (more potent analgesic; accumulates in renal failure); t½ 2–3 h. Uses: (1) analgesia — severe acute pain (trauma, burns, fractures, postoperative), myocardial infarction (relieves pain and anxiety, reduces preload), renal and biliary colic (with atropine; pethidine preferred historically), cancer pain (WHO ladder step 3; oral sustained-release), terminal care, labour (pethidine); (2) acute left ventricular failure — cardiac asthma/pulmonary oedema (venodilatation, reduced preload, relief of anxiety and dyspnoea); (3) preanaesthetic medication and balanced anaesthesia (fentanyl); (4) cough (codeine), diarrhoea (loperamide, diphenoxylate); (5) opioid dependence (methadone, buprenorphine substitution); (6) sedation in ventilated patients. Adverse effects: respiratory depression, nausea, vomiting, constipation, sedation, mental clouding, dysphoria, hallucinations, pruritus, urinary retention, postural hypotension, biliary colic, apnoea in newborn (if given during labour), tolerance (to analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression, nausea — not miosis and constipation), physical and psychological dependence, withdrawal syndrome (lacrimation, rhinorrhoea, yawning, mydriasis, piloerection, sweating, diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, anxiety, insomnia, tachycardia), opioid-induced hyperalgesia, hypogonadism; acute poisoning — triad of coma, pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression; cyanosis, hypotension, hypothermia, pulmonary oedema. Contraindications/precautions: head injury (respiratory depression raises PaCO2 and ICP; miosis masks pupillary signs; vomiting), bronchial asthma and COPD, hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, hypotension, hypovolaemia, liver disease (encephalopathy), renal failure (metabolite accumulation), elderly and infants (sensitive), biliary colic (spasm), undiagnosed acute abdomen, pregnancy and labour (neonatal respiratory depression), BPH, with MAO inhibitors (pethidine — serotonin syndrome), CNS depressants and alcohol (additive). Naloxone: pure competitive antagonist at μ, κ, δ receptors (highest affinity for μ); no agonist activity; poor oral bioavailability; given IV (onset 1–2 min), IM, SC, intranasal; short duration (30–90 min; t½ 1 h) — shorter than most opioids, so re-narcotisation can occur — repeat doses or infusion (two-thirds of reversal dose per hour); uses — acute opioid overdose (0.4–2 mg IV, repeat every 2–3 min up to 10 mg; titrate to adequate respiration), reversal of postoperative opioid respiratory depression (small doses 0.04–0.1 mg), neonatal respiratory depression from maternal opioids (10 µg/kg), diagnosis of opioid dependence (precipitates withdrawal), partial reversal of clonidine and alcohol toxicity (variable); precipitates acute withdrawal in dependent persons; may cause hypertension, tachycardia, pulmonary oedema, arrhythmias. Naltrexone: oral, long-acting antagonist for relapse prevention in opioid and alcohol dependence.",
[["Opioid receptors",[["Receptor","Effects"],["μ (MOP)","Analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression, miosis, constipation, dependence"],["κ (KOP)","Spinal analgesia, sedation, dysphoria, diuresis"],["δ (DOP)","Analgesia, mood"]]],
["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Natural","Morphine, codeine"],["Semisynthetic","Heroin, oxycodone, hydromorphone"],["Synthetic","Pethidine, fentanyl, methadone, tramadol, tapentadol"],["Agonist–antagonist","Pentazocine, butorphanol, nalbuphine"],["Partial agonist","Buprenorphine"],["Antagonists","Naloxone, naltrexone, nalmefene"]]],
["Uses of morphine",["Severe pain: trauma, burns, postoperative, cancer","Myocardial infarction","Acute LVF / pulmonary oedema","Preanaesthetic, balanced anaesthesia","Terminal care"]],
["Contraindications",["Head injury","Bronchial asthma, COPD","Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency","Hypotension, hypovolaemia","Liver/renal failure","Infants, elderly","Biliary colic, acute abdomen (undiagnosed)"]]],
{i:"Opioids remain the most effective analgesics for severe pain; their benefit must be balanced against respiratory depression, constipation, tolerance and dependence.",x:[["Opioid poisoning",["Triad: coma, pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression","Treatment: airway, ventilation, naloxone 0.4–2 mg IV repeated or infusion","Watch for re-narcotisation (naloxone short-acting)"]]],f:"Morphine → μ receptor (Gi)\n → ↓ cAMP, ↑ K⁺ efflux, ↓ Ca²⁺ influx → ↓ release of substance P/glutamate\n → spinal + supraspinal analgesia; descending inhibition\n Side effects: respiratory depression, miosis, constipation, vomiting, dependence\n Naloxone: competitive antagonist (short t½)",c:"Morphine provides powerful analgesia through μ-opioid receptors; careful dosing, monitoring for respiratory depression and availability of naloxone make opioid therapy safe."});

Z("pharm6",11,"ph",["Ethyl alcohol (ethanol) is a CNS depressant metabolised in the liver to acetaldehyde and acetate; chronic use damages liver, brain, heart and pancreas. Disulfiram blocks aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing unpleasant reactions when alcohol is drunk, helping to maintain abstinence.","Disulfiram doesn't stop the craving; it makes drinking feel awful — a deterrent alarm.",["Ethanol: GABA-A enhancement, NMDA inhibition; zero-order kinetics (ADH saturable, ~8–10 g/h); ↑ CYP2E1 (chronic).","Acute effects: disinhibition, euphoria, impaired judgement, ataxia, vasodilatation (hypothermia), diuresis (↓ ADH), hypoglycaemia; > 400 mg/dL — coma, death.","Chronic: fatty liver → hepatitis → cirrhosis, Wernicke–Korsakoff (thiamine), cardiomyopathy, pancreatitis, gastritis, neuropathy, FAS; withdrawal — tremor, seizures, DTs (BZDs).","Disulfiram: inhibits ALDH → acetaldehyde accumulates → flushing, throbbing headache, nausea, vomiting, hypotension, palpitations; dose 250–500 mg/day after 12 h abstinence; avoid alcohol-containing products; disulfiram-like drugs — metronidazole, cefoperazone, chlorpropamide, griseofulvin."],"Disulfiram-like drugs: 'Metronidazole, Cephalosporins (cefoperazone), Chlorpropamide, Griseofulvin'."],
"Ethyl alcohol (ethanol) is the most widely used psychoactive substance. Pharmacokinetics: rapidly absorbed from stomach and small intestine (faster on empty stomach, with carbonated drinks), distributed in total body water (lower volume in women — higher levels), crosses BBB and placenta; > 90% metabolised in the liver — by alcohol dehydrogenase (cytosolic; main pathway) to acetaldehyde, which is oxidised by aldehyde dehydrogenase (mitochondrial; ALDH2 deficiency in East Asians causes flushing) to acetate and then to CO2 and water; the microsomal ethanol oxidising system (CYP2E1) contributes at high concentrations and is induced in chronic drinkers; elimination follows zero-order kinetics (about 8–10 g or one standard drink per hour); small amounts excreted in breath (basis of breath analyser), urine and sweat; NADH excess produces lactic acidosis, hypoglycaemia, fatty liver, hyperuricaemia. Mechanism: enhances GABA-A receptor function, inhibits NMDA glutamate receptors, affects 5-HT3, glycine and nicotinic receptors, increases dopamine and endogenous opioids in reward pathway. Acute effects: CNS depressant — disinhibition, euphoria, impaired judgement and coordination, slurred speech, ataxia (blood alcohol 50–150 mg/dL), stupor (200–300), coma, respiratory depression and death (> 400–500 mg/dL); cutaneous vasodilatation (flushing, heat loss — hypothermia); diuresis (inhibits ADH); gastric irritation and increased acid secretion; hypoglycaemia (impairs gluconeogenesis); 'holiday heart' arrhythmias. Chronic effects: tolerance and dependence; liver — fatty liver, alcoholic hepatitis, cirrhosis, HCC; CNS — Wernicke encephalopathy and Korsakoff psychosis (thiamine deficiency), cerebellar degeneration, peripheral neuropathy, dementia, alcoholic hallucinosis; CVS — hypertension, dilated cardiomyopathy, arrhythmias (moderate intake possibly raises HDL); GI — gastritis, peptic ulcer, Mallory–Weiss tear, acute and chronic pancreatitis, malabsorption; haematological — macrocytosis, folate deficiency, thrombocytopenia; endocrine — pseudo-Cushing, hypogonadism, gynaecomastia; cancers (oral, oesophageal, liver, breast); fetal alcohol syndrome; drug interactions (enzyme induction chronically, inhibition acutely; additive CNS depression with sedatives). Uses: antiseptic (70% ethanol), solvent, counter-irritant and rubefacient, methanol and ethylene glycol poisoning (competitive ADH substrate), neurolysis (trigeminal neuralgia, celiac plexus block), appetite stimulation (historical). Alcohol withdrawal syndrome: tremors, sweating, anxiety, insomnia (6–24 h), hallucinations, seizures (12–48 h), delirium tremens (48–96 h; confusion, hallucinations, autonomic hyperactivity, potentially fatal) — treated with long-acting benzodiazepines (chlordiazepoxide, diazepam; lorazepam in liver disease), thiamine before glucose, fluids, electrolytes. Disulfiram (tetraethylthiuram disulphide): irreversibly inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase; if alcohol is ingested, acetaldehyde accumulates within 5–10 minutes causing the disulfiram–ethanol (acetaldehyde) reaction — flushing, throbbing headache, sweating, nausea, vomiting, thirst, chest pain, palpitations, tachycardia, hypotension, breathlessness, confusion, and in severe cases shock, arrhythmias, convulsions and death. Use: aversion therapy to maintain abstinence in motivated, compliant alcohol-dependent patients (preferably supervised); started at least 12–24 hours after last drink; dose 500 mg/day for 1–2 weeks then 125–250 mg/day; effect lasts up to 14 days after stopping. Patients must avoid alcohol in all forms (cough syrups, tinctures, mouthwashes, vinegar sauces, aftershave). Adverse effects: drowsiness, metallic/garlic taste, headache, rashes, impotence, hepatotoxicity, peripheral and optic neuropathy, psychosis; inhibits CYP — interactions with warfarin, phenytoin, metronidazole (psychosis). Contraindications: recent alcohol intake, cardiovascular disease, psychosis, severe liver disease, pregnancy. Other drugs for alcohol dependence: naltrexone (reduces craving and reward), acamprosate (NMDA modulation — reduces relapse), topiramate, baclofen, gabapentin, nalmefene. Disulfiram-like reaction is also produced by metronidazole, tinidazole, cefoperazone, cefotetan, chlorpropamide, griseofulvin, procarbazine, ketoconazole.",
[["Ethanol metabolism",["Ethanol → (ADH) → acetaldehyde → (ALDH) → acetate → CO2 + H2O","Zero-order kinetics (~8–10 g/h)","MEOS (CYP2E1) induced in chronic drinkers","Disulfiram blocks ALDH → acetaldehyde accumulates"]],
["Disulfiram reaction",["Flushing, throbbing headache","Nausea, vomiting, sweating","Tachycardia, hypotension, chest pain","Severe: shock, arrhythmia, convulsions"]],
["Drugs for alcohol dependence",[["Drug","Mechanism"],["Disulfiram","ALDH inhibition (aversion)"],["Naltrexone","Opioid antagonist (↓ craving/reward)"],["Acamprosate","NMDA modulation (↓ relapse)"],["Topiramate, baclofen","Off-label"],["Benzodiazepines","Withdrawal management"]]]],
{c:"Ethanol is a CNS depressant with zero-order kinetics and multi-organ toxicity; disulfiram deters drinking by causing acetaldehyde accumulation and is used with counselling in motivated patients."});

Z("pharm6",12,"ph",["Drugs used in de-addiction help people stop substances safely (detoxification), reduce craving and prevent relapse — for example methadone/buprenorphine for opioids, naltrexone/acamprosate/disulfiram for alcohol, and nicotine replacement/varenicline for smoking.","Detox helps the body get through withdrawal safely; maintenance and anti-craving drugs keep the person from slipping back, alongside counselling.",["Opioids: detox — tapering methadone/buprenorphine, clonidine/lofexidine for symptoms; maintenance — methadone (full agonist), buprenorphine ± naloxone (partial agonist, safer); relapse prevention — naltrexone; overdose — naloxone.","Alcohol: withdrawal — benzodiazepines (chlordiazepoxide, lorazepam) + thiamine; abstinence — disulfiram, naltrexone, acamprosate, topiramate, baclofen.","Nicotine: nicotine replacement (gum, patch, lozenge), varenicline (α4β2 partial agonist), bupropion; cytisine.","Others: cannabis, cocaine, amphetamines — no approved drugs; symptomatic and psychosocial therapy; benzodiazepine dependence — slow taper."],"Opioid maintenance: 'Methadone (full) vs Buprenorphine (partial, ceiling)'."],
"Pharmacotherapy of substance dependence aims at safe withdrawal (detoxification), reduction of craving, prevention of relapse, harm reduction and treatment of comorbidities, always combined with psychosocial interventions (motivational enhancement, cognitive behavioural therapy, self-help groups). Opioid dependence: detoxification — substitution with a long-acting opioid and gradual tapering (methadone, buprenorphine), or symptomatic treatment with α2 agonists clonidine/lofexidine (reduce autonomic withdrawal), with NSAIDs, antiemetics, loperamide, benzodiazepines; opioid substitution/maintenance therapy — methadone (oral, long-acting full μ agonist, once daily, prevents withdrawal and blocks euphoria of heroin; risk of QT prolongation, respiratory depression) and buprenorphine (sublingual partial μ agonist with ceiling effect on respiratory depression; combined with naloxone to deter injection; available in India under OST programme of NACO); relapse prevention — naltrexone (oral or depot; opioid antagonist blocking euphoria; start after complete detoxification, 7–10 days opioid-free); overdose — naloxone (including take-home intranasal kits). Alcohol dependence: withdrawal — long-acting benzodiazepines (chlordiazepoxide, diazepam; lorazepam/oxazepam in liver disease) by fixed or symptom-triggered schedule, thiamine (before glucose) to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy, multivitamins, fluids, electrolytes; maintenance of abstinence — disulfiram (aversive), naltrexone (reduces craving and heavy drinking), acamprosate (glutamate/NMDA modulator; reduces relapse; renal excretion), topiramate, baclofen, gabapentin, nalmefene (reduction). Nicotine dependence: nicotine replacement therapy (gum, patch, lozenge, inhaler, nasal spray — doubles quit rates), varenicline (α4β2 nicotinic partial agonist — reduces craving and withdrawal and blocks reward; most effective; nausea, vivid dreams), bupropion SR (NA/DA reuptake inhibitor; seizures at high dose), cytisine, nortriptyline, clonidine (second-line); counselling and quitline. Benzodiazepine dependence: switch to long-acting agent (diazepam, clonazepam) and taper slowly over weeks to months. Cannabis, cocaine, amphetamine dependence: no approved specific drugs; symptomatic (antipsychotics for psychosis, antidepressants), contingency management, CBT. Behavioural addictions and co-occurring psychiatric disorders need concurrent treatment. In India: Nasha Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan, Drug Treatment Clinics, NDPS Act provisions.",
[["Drugs by substance",[["Substance","Withdrawal","Maintenance/relapse prevention"],["Opioids","Methadone/buprenorphine taper, clonidine, lofexidine","Methadone, buprenorphine(+naloxone), naltrexone"],["Alcohol","Benzodiazepines + thiamine","Disulfiram, naltrexone, acamprosate, topiramate, baclofen"],["Nicotine","NRT","Varenicline, bupropion, NRT, cytisine"],["Benzodiazepines","Slow diazepam taper","—"],["Stimulants, cannabis","Symptomatic","Psychosocial"]]]],
{c:"Effective de-addiction combines pharmacotherapy for withdrawal, craving and relapse with psychosocial support; opioid substitution therapy and anti-craving drugs substantially improve outcomes."});

Z("pharm6",13,"ph",["Newer antiepileptics such as levetiracetam and lamotrigine are broad-spectrum, better tolerated and have fewer drug interactions than older drugs.","Levetiracetam fine-tunes neurotransmitter release from vesicles (SV2A); lamotrigine calms overactive sodium channels and glutamate release.",["Levetiracetam: binds SV2A synaptic vesicle protein → ↓ neurotransmitter release; broad spectrum (focal, GTCS, myoclonic), status epilepticus (IV), pregnancy-friendly; no hepatic metabolism, few interactions; ADR — behavioural changes (irritability, aggression, depression), somnolence.","Lamotrigine: blocks voltage-gated Na⁺ channels, ↓ glutamate release; focal, GTCS, absence, Lennox–Gastaut, bipolar depression (maintenance); safe in pregnancy (relatively).","Lamotrigine ADR: rash, SJS/TEN (risk ↑ with rapid titration and with valproate — halve dose), dizziness, diplopia, ataxia; levels ↑ by valproate, ↓ by enzyme inducers and OCPs.","Others: topiramate (weight loss, kidney stones, cognitive slowing), oxcarbazepine (hyponatraemia), lacosamide (PR prolongation), gabapentin/pregabalin (neuropathic pain), vigabatrin (infantile spasms; visual field loss), zonisamide, perampanel, brivaracetam."],"Lamotrigine = 'Low and slow' titration to avoid SJS."],
"Newer (second- and third-generation) antiepileptic drugs offer broad spectrum activity, improved tolerability, fewer drug interactions and better safety in special populations. Levetiracetam: mechanism — binds synaptic vesicle glycoprotein 2A (SV2A), modulating vesicle fusion and neurotransmitter release; also inhibits N-type Ca²⁺ channels; pharmacokinetics — rapid and complete oral absorption, linear kinetics, < 10% protein bound, not metabolised by CYP (hydrolysis), renal excretion (adjust in renal failure), t½ 6–8 h; IV and oral formulations; uses — focal seizures (mono- and adjunctive), generalised tonic–clonic seizures, myoclonic seizures (juvenile myoclonic epilepsy), status epilepticus (second-line IV), seizure prophylaxis in neurosurgery and head injury, preferred in pregnancy and women of childbearing potential (low teratogenic risk), in elderly and patients on multiple drugs; adverse effects — somnolence, fatigue, dizziness, behavioural and psychiatric effects (irritability, aggression, anxiety, depression, suicidal ideation — 'Keppra rage'; pyridoxine may help), rarely psychosis, rash. Brivaracetam is a higher-affinity SV2A ligand. Lamotrigine: mechanism — blocks voltage-gated Na⁺ channels (stabilising inactivated state), inhibits release of glutamate and aspartate, and may block Ca²⁺ channels; pharmacokinetics — well absorbed, hepatic glucuronidation, t½ 24–30 h (halved by enzyme inducers, doubled by valproate; oestrogen-containing contraceptives and pregnancy lower levels); uses — focal and generalised tonic–clonic seizures (mono- and add-on), absence seizures (second-line), Lennox–Gastaut syndrome, bipolar disorder (maintenance; prevents depressive episodes), relatively safe in pregnancy (dose adjustments needed); does not cause weight gain or cognitive impairment; adverse effects — dizziness, diplopia, ataxia, headache, nausea, insomnia, skin rash (up to 10%) and serious cutaneous reactions (Stevens–Johnson syndrome, TEN, DRESS — higher in children, with rapid dose escalation and co-administration with valproate; start low, titrate slowly over 6–8 weeks, stop at first sign of rash), aseptic meningitis, haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis; may worsen myoclonic seizures. Other newer AEDs: topiramate (multiple mechanisms; also migraine prophylaxis, obesity; ADR — cognitive slowing, word-finding difficulty, paraesthesia, weight loss, renal stones, angle-closure glaucoma, metabolic acidosis, teratogenic cleft lip), oxcarbazepine (fewer interactions than carbamazepine; hyponatraemia), lacosamide (slow Na⁺ inactivation; IV; PR prolongation), gabapentin and pregabalin (α2δ ligands; mainly neuropathic pain, adjunct for focal seizures; sedation, weight gain, oedema, misuse potential), vigabatrin (irreversible GABA transaminase inhibitor; infantile spasms with tuberous sclerosis; irreversible visual field constriction), tiagabine, zonisamide, rufinamide, perampanel (AMPA antagonist), cenobamate, felbamate (aplastic anaemia), cannabidiol (Dravet and Lennox–Gastaut syndromes), fenfluramine (Dravet), stiripentol, everolimus (TSC).",
[["Newer AEDs",[["Drug","Mechanism","Key ADR / feature"],["Levetiracetam","SV2A binding","Behavioural changes; safe in pregnancy; few interactions"],["Lamotrigine","Na⁺ channel block, ↓ glutamate","Rash/SJS (titrate slowly); bipolar depression"],["Topiramate","Na⁺, GABA, AMPA, CA inhibition","Cognitive slowing, weight loss, stones"],["Oxcarbazepine","Na⁺ channel block","Hyponatraemia"],["Lacosamide","Slow Na⁺ inactivation","PR prolongation"],["Gabapentin/pregabalin","α2δ Ca²⁺ subunit","Sedation; neuropathic pain"],["Vigabatrin","GABA-T inhibition","Visual field loss; infantile spasms"]]],
["Levetiracetam vs lamotrigine",[["Feature","Levetiracetam","Lamotrigine"],["Titration","Rapid","Slow (rash risk)"],["Interactions","Minimal","Valproate ↑, inducers/OCP ↓"],["Status epilepticus","IV available","No"],["Psychiatric effect","Irritability, depression","Mood stabilising"],["Pregnancy","Preferred","Preferred (adjust dose)"]]]],
{c:"Levetiracetam and lamotrigine are broad-spectrum, well-tolerated antiepileptics favoured in pregnancy and polytherapy; lamotrigine needs slow titration and levetiracetam monitoring for mood changes."});
